var e={updated:`2026-11-26`,cars:[{makeSlug:`toyota`,modelSlug:`camry`,make:`Toyota`,model:`Camry`,years:`2002–2026`,codes:[`85D23L`,`DIN66L`,`85D23R`,`LN2`],startStopNote:!1,kind:`general`,evidence:`Nam Wah Battery Co Pte Ltd Vehicle Application Guide (Amaron's Singapore distributor).`,fitment:[{version:`Camry 2.4i Altise / Ateva / Grande / Sportivo / Touring (ACV36 - ACV40)`,years:`09/2002-2011`,guide:`FLO 85D23L`,code:`85D23L`},{version:`Camry 3.0i Altise / Ateva / Azura / Grande / Sportivo (MCV36R)`,years:`09/2002-2011`,guide:`FLO 85D23L`,code:`85D23L`},{version:`Camry 2.5i (ASV50)`,years:`12/2011-2017`,guide:`FLO 85D23L`,code:`85D23L`},{version:`Camry 2.5i (ASV70)`,years:`09/2017-on`,guide:`PRO DIN66L`,code:`DIN66L`},{version:`Camry 3.5i V6 (GSV70)`,years:`09/2017-on`,guide:`PRO DIN66L`,code:`DIN66L`},{version:`Camry Hybrid series 1 2.4i/105kW (AHV40)`,years:`07/2009-2011`,guide:`FLO 85D23R`,code:`85D23R`},{version:`Camry Hybrid series II 2.5i/105kW (AVV50)`,years:`01/2012-2017`,guide:`FLO 85D23R`,code:`85D23R`},{version:`Camry Hybrid series III 2.5i / 88kW (AXVH71)`,years:`09/2017-on`,guide:`JADE AGM L2`,code:`LN2`}]},{makeSlug:`toyota`,modelSlug:`corolla-altis`,make:`Toyota`,model:`Corolla Altis`,years:`2007–2026`,codes:[`60B24LS`,`85D23L`,`42B20R`,`DIN55L`,`DIN66L`],startStopNote:!1,kind:`general`,evidence:`Nam Wah Battery Co Pte Ltd Vehicle Application Guide (Amaron's Singapore distributor).`,fitment:[{version:`Corolla ZRE152R Ascent / Conquest / Edge / Levin / Ultima / ZR (ZRE152R)`,years:`05/2007-2013`,guide:`FLO 55B24LS`,code:`60B24LS`},{version:`Corolla ZRE172R Sedan (ZRE172R)`,years:`2013-2019`,guide:`FLO 55B24LS`,code:`60B24LS`},{version:`Corolla ZRE182R Hatch (ZRE182R)`,years:`10/2012-2019`,guide:`FLO 85D23L`,code:`85D23L`},{version:`Corolla ZWE186R 1.8/60kW Hybrid (check polarity) (ZWE186R)`,years:`2016-2018`,guide:`FLO 42B20R`,code:`42B20R`},{version:`Corolla ZWE211R 1.8/53kW Hybrid (check polarity) (ZWE211R)`,years:`06/2018-on`,guide:`PRO DIN55L`,code:`DIN55L`},{version:`Corolla MZEA 2.0i (MZEA12R)`,years:`06/2018-on`,guide:`PRO DIN66L`,code:`DIN66L`}]},{makeSlug:`toyota`,modelSlug:`prius`,make:`Toyota`,model:`Prius`,years:`2003–2026`,codes:[`42B20R`,`60B24R`,`DIN45L`],startStopNote:!1,kind:`hybrid`,evidence:`Nam Wah Battery Co Pte Ltd Vehicle Application Guide (Amaron's Singapore distributor).`,fitment:[{version:`Prius 2nd Gen 1.5i/50kW - no Smart-Key system (NHW20R)`,years:`10/2003-2009`,guide:`FLO 42B20R`,code:`42B20R`},{version:`Prius 2nd Gen 1.5i/50kW i-Tech - fitted with Smart-Key system (NHW20R)`,years:`10/2003-2009`,guide:`FLO 55B24R`,code:`60B24R`},{version:`Prius 3rd Gen 1.8i/60kW (ZVW30R)`,years:`07/2009-2017`,guide:`FLO 55B24R`,code:`60B24R`},{version:`Prius (ZBW50R)`,years:`2016-on`,guide:`PRO DIN45L`,code:`DIN45L`},{version:`Prius C 1.5i/45kW Compact (NHP10R)`,years:`2011-2018`,guide:`FLO 55B24R`,code:`60B24R`},{version:`Prius V 1.8i/60kW Wagon (ZVW40R)`,years:`2012-2018`,guide:`FLO 55B24R`,code:`60B24R`}]}]},t={updated:`2026-11-26`,guides:[{slug:`battery-size-codes-explained`,title:`Car Battery Codes Explained: 55B24, 80D26, 105D31 and DIN66`,short:`What the numbers and letters on your battery mean`,description:`What battery codes such as 55B24, 80D26, 105D31, DIN66 and LN3 actually mean, why the last letter L or R matters, and how to be sure a replacement fits.`,lead:`The code printed on your battery is not random. It tells you the size, the rough power rating and which side the positive terminal sits on. Here is how to read it.`,sections:[{h:`Japanese-size codes: 55B24L, 80D26L, 105D31R`,p:[`Most Japanese and Korean cars, and many cars sold in Singapore, use a battery code in the JIS style. A code such as 80D26L has four parts.`,`The first number (80) is a performance rating. It is a relative figure, not a capacity in amp-hours. In the same case size, a bigger number means more starting power. The letter (D) is a size group for the width and height of the case. The second number (26) is the approximate length of the case in centimetres. The last letter (L or R) tells you which side the positive terminal is on.`]},{h:`Why the last letter matters`,p:[`A 55B24L and a 55B24R are the same size and rating. The difference is that the terminals are on opposite ends. If you fit the wrong one, the cables may not reach, or they may be stretched across the battery, which is unsafe. When you replace a battery, the positive terminal must be in the same position as on your old battery. Check this on your old battery before you buy.`]},{h:`Two batteries with similar codes are not always interchangeable`,p:[`A bigger first number in the same case size is usually fine, because the battery is simply stronger. A different size group letter or length number means the case is a different size and may not fit the tray or the hold-down clamp. Terminal height and the vent layout can also differ between brands, so a photo of your old battery helps us confirm the match.`]},{h:`European codes: DIN66, DIN70, LN3 and similar`,p:[`Many European cars, and some newer cars of other makes, use European-style codes. DIN66, DIN70 and DIN77 describe both the size and the capacity class. LN2, LN3 and LN4 are another European size series. The terminal position is built into these sizes, so you do not choose left or right the way you do with Japanese codes.`,`European cars are more likely to use AGM or EFB batteries and to need the new battery registered with the car's computer. See our guides on that below.`]},{h:`What to do if you cannot read the code`,p:[`Batteries get dirty, and labels fade. Wipe the label with a dry cloth, take a photo in good light, and send it to us together with your car model and year. You do not have to understand the code yourself. We will read it and confirm the right replacement with you.`],list:[`Photograph the whole label, not only the code.`,`Show the side with the two terminals, so we can see where the positive (+) terminal is.`,`Note any words such as EFB, AGM or Start-Stop.`]}],faqs:[{q:`Does the first number on the battery code mean amp-hours?`,a:`No. In Japanese-style codes such as 80D26L, the first number is a performance rating, not a capacity in amp-hours. Compare it only between batteries of the same case size.`},{q:`Can I use a battery with a higher first number?`,a:`Often yes, if the size group letter, the length and the terminal side are the same. The battery is then stronger, not different. Send us your label and we will confirm the match.`},{q:`What if my battery has no L or R?`,a:`European sizes such as DIN66 or LN3 do not use L and R. For other codes, send a photo of the terminals and we will check which side the positive terminal is on.`}]},{slug:`efb-agm-or-standard-battery`,title:`EFB, AGM or Standard Battery? Why Stop-Start Cars Cannot Go Cheaper`,short:`Which battery type your car needs`,description:`The difference between standard, EFB and AGM car batteries, how to tell which type your car needs, and why a stop-start car must not be fitted with a plain standard battery.`,lead:`If your car shuts its engine off at traffic lights, the battery is doing far more work than in an ordinary car. That is why the type matters.`,sections:[{h:`The three types in plain words`,p:[`A standard (flooded or maintenance-free) battery is built for starting the engine and running the electrics of an ordinary car. An EFB (enhanced flooded battery) is a stronger flooded design for cars with basic stop-start. An AGM (absorbent glass mat) battery is sealed, handles repeated deep discharge and fast recharging better, and is used in many cars with stop-start, regenerative braking and heavy electrical loads.`]},{h:`Why stop-start cars need EFB or AGM`,p:[`A stop-start car restarts the engine many times in a single trip, and the battery also powers the air-conditioning, lights and screens while the engine is off. A plain standard battery wears out much faster under this load. It can also cause the stop-start system to stop working, because the car's computer will not stop the engine when it thinks the battery is weak.`]},{h:`The rule: same type or better, never lower`,p:[`If the label on your old battery says EFB, the replacement can be EFB or AGM. If it says AGM, the replacement must be AGM. A standard battery is never an acceptable replacement for either. A cheaper battery of the wrong type may work for a few weeks and then fail early, so it is not a saving.`]},{h:`How to tell what your car needs`,p:[`The most reliable way is the label on your current battery. It usually says EFB or AGM if it is one of those types. Also look at the car itself:`],list:[`Does the engine switch off at traffic lights and restart when you lift off the brake? Then the car has stop-start.`,`Is there an Auto Stop, i-stop, Idle Stop or Start-Stop button or light on the dashboard?`,`Is the battery in the boot or under a seat, instead of under the bonnet? Many European cars do this and use AGM.`]},{h:`If you are not sure`,p:[`Send a photo of the label on your current battery and tell us your car model and year. If we cannot be certain, we will ask the person in charge to confirm before anything is quoted.`]}],faqs:[{q:`Can I replace an AGM battery with an EFB battery?`,a:`No. An AGM battery should be replaced with an AGM battery. An EFB battery can be replaced with EFB or AGM.`},{q:`My car has no stop-start. Do I still need EFB or AGM?`,a:`Not unless the label on your old battery says so. A car without stop-start and with a standard battery can use a standard replacement of the correct size.`},{q:`Will a standard battery damage my stop-start car?`,a:`It is unlikely to cause lasting damage, but it will wear out faster, and the stop-start system may be switched off by the car. The type on the old label is the guide.`}]},{slug:`car-wont-start-battery-or-not`,title:`Car Won't Start? How to Tell If It Is the Battery`,short:`A safe checklist before you call anyone`,description:`How to tell if a car that will not start has a flat or failing battery, the safe things to check yourself, and when to stop and ask for help.`,lead:`A car that will not start is stressful. A few quick, safe checks will tell you whether the battery is the likely cause.`,sections:[{h:`First, make sure you and the car are safe`,p:[`If the car has stopped on the road, turn on the hazard lights, get everyone out of traffic, and stay away from moving cars. Do not open the bonnet in a live traffic lane. Do not smoke or use a flame near a battery.`]},{h:`Signs that point to the battery`,p:[`A weak or flat battery usually shows itself in one of these ways:`],list:[`When you turn the key or press the start button, there is a fast clicking sound, or nothing at all.`,`The engine turns over very slowly and then gives up.`,`The dashboard lights and the screen are dim, flicker, or do not come on.`,`The headlights are weak, or they dim sharply when you try to start.`,`The car started fine yesterday, but it has sat for several days, or something stayed on (interior light, a dashcam in parking mode).`]},{h:`Signs that point to something else`,p:[`If the engine cranks strongly at normal speed but does not catch, the battery is probably fine. The cause is more likely fuel, spark or a sensor. If you hear a single loud click and the lights stay bright, the starter motor may be the problem. If the car has just run out of fuel, no battery will help.`]},{h:`Safe things you can check`,p:[`Switch off every electrical load (lights, air-conditioning, radio) and try again once. Do not keep cranking the engine, because that drains a weak battery and can overheat the starter. If you can reach the battery safely, look for loose or heavily corroded terminals, a cracked case or a swollen case. A cracked or leaking battery must not be jump-started or touched.`]},{h:`A note about jump-starting`,p:[`Jump-starting is risky on modern cars. Many have sensitive electronics, and hybrid and European cars often have specific jump points and rules. Follow your owner's manual. If you are not completely sure how to do it, do not try.`]},{h:`When to ask for help`,p:[`If the battery is old (more than three years), if the checks above point to the battery, or if you are not sure, send us a message on WhatsApp with your location, your car model and year, and a photo of the battery label if you can reach it safely. Our reply is not instant, because the person in charge replies personally, so if you are in a dangerous spot, call your roadside assistance or the emergency services first.`]}],faqs:[{q:`Can a battery be dead even though the car started yesterday?`,a:`Yes. An old battery can fail without warning, and a battery can also be drained overnight by a light, a faulty switch or a dashcam in parking mode.`},{q:`Will driving for 20 minutes recharge a flat battery?`,a:`Only partly. A short drive does not restore a deeply drained battery, and a battery that keeps going flat usually needs to be tested or replaced.`},{q:`Should I disconnect the battery myself?`,a:`We do not recommend it. On many modern cars, disconnecting the battery can reset settings and cause warnings. Ask for help first.`}]},{slug:`how-long-car-batteries-last-singapore`,title:`Car Battery Lifespan in Singapore: How Long It Lasts and When to Replace It`,short:`How long to expect, how to check it, and when to replace`,description:`How long a car battery lasts in Singapore's heat, how to check it with a simple voltage test, the warning signs of a failing battery, and when to replace it.`,lead:`There is no fixed expiry date for a car battery. In Singapore's climate and traffic a few things shorten its life, and there are simple checks that tell you how much life is left before the car leaves you stranded.`,sections:[{h:`How long does a car battery last in Singapore?`,p:[`A typical car battery lasts about two to four years. Some last longer and some fail sooner. This is a rule of thumb, not a guarantee. How long yours lasts depends on the battery type, how the car is used, how often it sits unused, and the condition of the car's charging system.`,`Batteries do not fade gradually in a way you always notice. A battery that starts the car fine this week can fail suddenly after a few days of parking or one very short trip, which is why checking the age and the signs matters more than waiting for a failure.`]},{h:`Why Singapore is hard on batteries`,p:[`Heat speeds up the chemical wear inside a battery, and Singapore is warm all year. Many cars here also do mostly short trips in stop-and-go traffic. A short trip does not give the alternator enough time to fully recharge the battery after starting, so the battery slowly runs lower and lower. Cars that sit unused for days, and cars with a dashcam in parking mode, add to the problem.`,`A battery that is often left only partly charged wears out faster than one that is regularly topped up by a long, steady drive.`]},{h:`How to find out how old your battery is`,p:[`Many batteries carry a date code on the case or on a sticker, but the format differs between brands, so it is not always obvious. If the code is unclear, check your invoice or ask the workshop that fitted it. If you bought the car second-hand and do not know when the battery was last changed, assume it is the original and treat it as three years or older once the car itself is that old.`]},{h:`A simple home check with a multimeter`,p:[`If you own a multimeter you can get a rough idea of the state of charge. Measure across the two battery terminals when the car has been switched off for several hours.`,`The usual reading guide for a standard lead-acid battery at rest is below. Treat it as a rough guide only. A workshop tester is more reliable because it also tests how the battery behaves under load.`],list:[`About 12.6 volts or higher: the battery is close to fully charged.`,`About 12.4 volts: roughly three-quarters charged.`,`About 12.2 volts: roughly half charged. Recharge it and see whether it holds.`,`Below about 12.0 volts: badly discharged. If it will not recover after a full charge, it is likely worn out.`]},{h:`Is it the battery or the charging system?`,p:[`A flat battery is not always a faulty battery. If the battery keeps going flat even after a long drive, the alternator or its belt may not be charging it. With the engine running, the voltage across the terminals is usually a bit above 13 volts and below about 15 volts. A reading near the resting voltage while the engine is running points to a charging problem, not the battery. A workshop can test both in a few minutes.`]},{h:`Warning signs`,p:[`Most batteries give some warning before they fail. Watch for these signs:`],list:[`The engine cranks more slowly than it used to, especially on the first start of the day.`,`The headlights and interior lights dim when you start the car or run the air-conditioning.`,`The battery or charging warning light comes on.`,`The case is swollen, or there is white or blue crust around the terminals.`,`The stop-start system stops working, or the car refuses to switch off at lights.`,`The battery is more than three years old and has never been tested.`]},{h:`Standard, EFB and AGM batteries`,p:[`Cars with automatic engine stop-start use EFB or AGM batteries, which are built for repeated starting. They can last as long as or somewhat longer than a standard battery when the car is driven normally, but they are not immune to heat and short trips. Whatever the type, the replacement has to match the type your car was built for.`]},{h:`Habits that help`,p:[`Take the car for a longer drive from time to time. Switch off the lights and the air-conditioning before you turn off the engine. Check that the dashcam is wired with a low-voltage cut-off. Keep the terminals clean and tight. If the car sits unused for weeks, ask a workshop about keeping the battery charged.`]},{h:`When to replace it`,p:[`A battery that fails in a car park or on a highway costs more in time and stress than one replaced a little early. If your battery is more than three years old and the car is showing signs, replace it before the next long trip. Send us a photo of the label and your car model on WhatsApp and we will confirm the right replacement.`]}],faqs:[{q:`Can I just test my battery to see if it is still good?`,a:`A workshop can test a battery's health with a tester. The result is a good guide, but a battery that is already old can still fail soon after a pass.`},{q:`How many years should a car battery last?`,a:`About two to four years is a common range in Singapore. It can be shorter with many short trips, long periods parked, or a dashcam that keeps drawing power.`},{q:`Does a new battery need a break-in period?`,a:`No. A new battery is ready to use. Long, steady drives in the first weeks help it reach a full charge.`},{q:`Will a bigger battery last longer?`,a:`Not if it does not fit. Use the size and type on your old label. A larger capacity in the same case size can be fine, but the case must match.`},{q:`My battery is flat again after a few days. Is it dead?`,a:`Not necessarily. Something may be draining it while the car is parked, or the car may not be charging it while driving. See our guides on a battery draining overnight and a battery that is not charging.`}]},{slug:`european-car-battery-registration`,title:`Why European Cars May Need Battery Registration (Coding)`,short:`What to know before changing a BMW, Mercedes, Audi or VW battery`,description:`Why many European cars, such as BMW, Mercedes-Benz, Audi and Volkswagen, need a new battery to be registered with the car's computer, and what can go wrong if it is skipped.`,lead:`On many European cars, swapping the battery is not just a spanner job. The car's computer wants to be told that a new battery has been fitted.`,sections:[{h:`What battery registration is`,p:[`Many European cars, including many BMW, Mercedes-Benz, Audi, Volkswagen, Volvo, MINI and Land Rover models, manage the charging of the battery with a computer. The computer tracks the battery's age, type and capacity, and it adjusts how hard the alternator charges it. When a new battery is fitted, the computer must be told, using a diagnostic tool. This is often called registering or coding the battery.`]},{h:`What can happen if you skip it`,p:[`If the new battery is not registered, the car may keep charging it as if it were old and worn. That can shorten the new battery's life. Some cars also show warnings, switch off the stop-start system, or limit comfort features to save power.`]},{h:`The battery is often AGM or EFB, and in an unusual place`,p:[`Many European cars use AGM or EFB batteries. The battery may be in the boot, under a seat or under the floor. The new battery must be the same type or better and the right size, including its venting arrangement, which is why a standard battery is not a safe substitute. See our guide to EFB and AGM batteries.`]},{h:`Not every European car is the same`,p:[`The need for registration depends on the make, the model and the year. Some older European cars do not need it. Newer ones usually do. This is why a general answer is not enough, and why we ask for your car model and year, as well as a photo of the battery label.`]},{h:`How we handle European cars`,p:[`We do not quote a fixed price for European cars straight away. We collect your car model, year, location and a photo of the battery label, and the person in charge confirms the right battery and how the replacement will be done. The reply is from a person, not instant.`]}],faqs:[{q:`Can I change the battery in my BMW or Mercedes myself?`,a:`It is physically possible on some models, but a new battery usually needs to be registered with the car's computer. Without that, the battery may wear out early.`},{q:`Does registration apply to Japanese cars too?`,a:`Most Japanese cars do not need it. Some newer models with advanced charging control may. Send us your car model and year.`},{q:`What about electric cars and hybrids?`,a:`They have a separate 12V battery that powers the car's systems. It is often a special type, and the procedure can be different. We confirm each case with the person in charge.`}]},{slug:`warranty-questions-for-car-batteries`,title:`Car Battery Warranty in Singapore: What It Covers and What to Ask Before You Buy`,short:`What a battery warranty usually covers`,description:`What a car battery warranty covers and excludes, free replacement vs pro-rata, how claims are tested, why they get rejected, and what to ask before buying.`,lead:`A warranty is only useful if you know what it covers and how to claim. These are the questions worth asking before you pay for any car battery, and what to expect if you ever need to claim.`,sections:[{h:`What a battery warranty usually covers`,p:[`A battery warranty generally covers manufacturing defects, such as an internal fault that makes the battery fail early under normal use. It usually does not cover damage from misuse or from a fault elsewhere in the car.`]},{h:`Free replacement or pro-rata`,p:[`Warranties are usually one of two kinds. A free replacement warranty gives you a new battery at no charge if the battery fails inside the period. A pro-rata warranty gives you a discount on a new battery that shrinks the longer you have used the old one. Some warranties start with a free replacement period and then turn into pro-rata. Ask which kind you are getting, because the same number of years can mean very different things.`]},{h:`What it usually does not cover`,p:[`Typical exclusions are a cracked or damaged case, a battery that has been deeply drained repeatedly, a faulty alternator or electrical fault that damages the battery, incorrect installation, and wear from normal ageing. A battery of the wrong type for the car may also be excluded.`]},{h:`How a warranty claim is usually checked`,p:[`When you bring a battery back, the seller or the maker normally checks it before agreeing to replace it. A typical check looks at the label and date, the physical condition of the case and terminals, the battery's charge level, and how it behaves under a load test. Some sellers also test the car's charging system, because a faulty alternator can kill a good battery. If the test shows the battery is fine, the fault is probably elsewhere.`]},{h:`Why claims get rejected`,p:[`Most rejected claims come from a short list of reasons:`],list:[`No receipt or other proof of the date of purchase or replacement.`,`The label has been removed, damaged or cannot be read.`,`The case is cracked, swollen from heat damage, or has been opened.`,`The battery has been left flat for a long time, or deeply discharged again and again.`,`A fault in the car's charging system damaged it.`,`It was the wrong type for the car, for example a standard battery fitted where the car needed an EFB or AGM.`]},{h:`Questions to ask the seller`,p:[`Ask these before you agree to buy, and ask for the answers in writing in the chat or on the receipt:`],list:[`How long is the warranty, and does it start from the day of replacement?`,`Is it a free replacement for the whole period, or does the value reduce over time?`,`What do I need to bring to make a claim? (Usually the receipt and the battery.)`,`Who will test the battery, and how long does the process take?`,`Is the warranty the same for standard, EFB and AGM batteries? Warranty periods often differ by type.`]},{h:`Keep your proof`,p:[`Keep the receipt or the WhatsApp conversation, and note the date of replacement. Do not remove or damage the battery label, because the claim is checked against the code on it.`]},{h:`Warranty is not the same as lifespan`,p:[`A battery that lasts well beyond its warranty is normal, and a battery can also fail just after the warranty ends. The warranty period tells you what the seller or maker will stand behind, not how long the battery will actually last. See how long a car battery lasts in Singapore for what shortens battery life.`]},{h:`The old battery`,p:[`Ask what happens to your old battery. In a proper replacement, the old battery is taken away for recycling, and you should not leave it on the road or in a bin. Car batteries contain lead and acid and must be recycled.`]},{h:`How we handle it`,p:[`The warranty depends on the battery. We tell you the warranty period for the battery we quote, and the person in charge confirms how to make a claim. We do not make promises beyond what the battery's warranty covers.`]}],faqs:[{q:`Does the warranty start when I buy or when I fit?`,a:`Ask the seller. For a replacement done at the same time, they are usually the same day. Always keep the receipt or chat as proof of the date.`},{q:`Is a longer warranty always better?`,a:`Not necessarily. Check what is covered and how a claim works. A long warranty with a difficult claim process is worth less than a shorter one that is simple to use.`},{q:`What if my battery fails because of the alternator?`,a:`A fault in the car's charging system is usually not covered by the battery's warranty. A test will show where the fault is.`},{q:`Do I need the receipt to claim?`,a:`Almost always. The receipt, invoice or a dated chat message shows when the battery was fitted. Keep it somewhere you will find it, such as a photo in your phone.`},{q:`Is the warranty the same for every battery?`,a:`No. The period and the terms depend on the battery and its type. Ask for the terms of the exact battery you are buying.`}]},{slug:`forklift-lorry-and-factory-vehicle-batteries`,title:`Lorry and Forklift Battery Replacement in Singapore: Before You Order`,short:`Lorry, forklift and work vehicle batteries: what to check and send`,description:`Lorry, truck and engine forklift battery replacement in Singapore: 12V or 24V, why batteries fail early, warning signs and what to send for a quote.`,lead:`Planning a lorry battery replacement, or new starter batteries for forklifts, trucks and other commercial vehicles in Singapore? Check three things before you order: the system voltage, how many batteries the vehicle uses, and the code on the label of each one. Then send us photos for a quote.`,waMessage:`Hi SG Tyre Hub, I need batteries for work vehicles (forklift, lorry or fleet). I'll send the vehicle details and a photo of a battery label.`,sections:[{h:`What this guide covers`,p:[`Engine-powered forklifts (diesel or LPG), lorries, vans, buses and other work vehicles start on a 12V or 24V starter battery, much like a car, only larger. This guide is about those. Electric forklifts run on a large traction battery pack. That is a different product, so please ask separately instead of assuming the same answer.`]},{h:`Lorry, truck and forklift batteries: check the label first`,p:[`Do not order from the vehicle model alone. Vehicles may have been fitted with a different battery since they were new. Read the code on the battery that is in the vehicle now, check that the case size and terminal positions match, and note whether there are two batteries. Our guide to [battery size codes](/batteries/guides/battery-size-codes-explained/) explains what the letters and numbers mean.`]},{h:`How a work vehicle differs from a car`,p:[`Many trucks and forklifts are 24V and use two 12V batteries connected in series. If you replace only one, the pair will be mismatched and the new one will wear out early, so both batteries should be checked together. Case sizes are often larger than car sizes, and the battery sits in a tray or box that has to match.`,`Constant vibration, heavy loads, dust and long idling hours shorten battery life, so the type of battery matters more than for a car.`]},{h:`12V or 24V: how to tell`,p:[`The label on the vehicle or in the manual usually states the system voltage. A simple visual clue is two batteries side by side joined by a short cable, with the vehicle's main leads taken from the two outer terminals. That is usually a 24V system made of two 12V batteries. Do not guess. Send us a photo and we will confirm.`]},{h:`Why work vehicle batteries fail early`,p:[`The same few causes come up again and again on forklifts and lorries:`],list:[`Vibration loosens the battery in its tray and damages the plates inside. A hold-down that is loose or missing is a common cause.`,`Long idling and short, frequent starts mean the battery is rarely fully recharged.`,`Added equipment such as lights, radios, trackers or cameras draws power even when the engine is off.`,`Corroded or loose terminals and cables cause weak starting that looks like a bad battery.`,`A faulty alternator undercharges or overcharges the battery and wears it out quickly.`,`Mixing an old battery with a new one in a 24V pair puts extra load on the new battery.`]},{h:`If the battery keeps going flat`,p:[`A new battery will not cure a charging fault or something draining it. Our guides explain [charging faults](/batteries/guides/car-battery-not-charging-alternator-belt-or-battery/), [overnight drain](/batteries/guides/car-battery-draining-overnight-causes/) and [terminal corrosion](/batteries/guides/car-battery-terminal-corrosion-causes-and-cleaning/) for background. These guides are written for 12V cars. Their voltage readings and test and cleaning steps do not carry over directly to a 24V vehicle with two batteries. Have a trained person test it.`]},{h:`Signs it is time to replace the batteries`,p:[`Work vehicles usually give warning before they fail:`],list:[`The engine cranks slowly, especially first thing in the morning or after the vehicle has stood overnight.`,`The vehicle needs a jump start more than once.`,`The lights dim when the starter turns.`,`The battery case is swollen, leaking or has crust around the terminals.`,`The batteries are several years old and have never been tested.`]},{h:`Before anyone touches the batteries`,p:[`Work vehicle batteries can weigh well over 20 kg each and contain lead and acid. Switch the vehicle off, remove the key, and disconnect the negative lead first. Use proper lifting equipment and protective gloves and eyewear. If you are not sure, leave it to someone trained. The old batteries must be recycled, not left in a bin or on the road.`]},{h:`What to send us`,p:[`The more of this you send, the faster and more accurate the quote is:`],list:[`The type of vehicle and the make and model, for example a Toyota 8FD forklift or a 10-foot lorry.`,`The voltage, 12V or 24V, and how many batteries the vehicle has.`,`A clear photo of the label on each battery, and one photo of where the battery sits.`,`How many vehicles need batteries, and whether they can be done together.`,`Where the vehicles are, and when someone can let us in.`]},{h:`How we quote`,p:[`For factories and fleets, the person in charge prepares the quote personally. We do not give an instant price for a job like this, because the right battery, the number of units and the location all change the answer. A call-out charge for coming to your site can apply, and we tell you the full amount before anything is arranged.`]},{h:`Doing several vehicles at once`,p:[`If you have a few vehicles that are due, it usually makes sense to do them in one visit. Tell us the count and the models when you first write, and we can plan one trip instead of several.`]}],faqs:[{q:`Do you replace batteries at our factory?`,a:`We can arrange a visit to your site. The person in charge confirms the details and the call-out charge with you in advance.`},{q:`Can you supply batteries for electric forklifts?`,a:`Electric forklifts use a large traction battery, which is a different product. Ask us, and the person in charge will tell you whether we can help.`},{q:`Is there a price for fleet orders?`,a:`Every job is quoted individually, based on the batteries needed, the number of units and the location. Send the vehicle details and we will quote.`},{q:`Can I replace just one battery in a 24V forklift or lorry?`,a:`It is better to check both. If one of the pair is old and the other new, the new one carries extra load and wears out early. A tester will show whether the older one is still good.`},{q:`How long do forklift and lorry batteries last?`,a:`There is no fixed number. Vibration, idling, equipment left on and the state of the charging system all change it, so test the batteries regularly instead of waiting for a failure.`},{q:`What do you need to quote a lorry battery replacement?`,a:`The vehicle make and model, 12V or 24V, how many batteries it has, a clear photo of each battery label and of where they sit, and where the vehicle is.`}]},{slug:`disconnect-car-battery-before-overseas-trip`,title:`Going Overseas? Should You Disconnect Your Car Battery?`,short:`What to do with your battery before a long trip away`,description:`Leaving your car parked while you travel? How long a healthy battery can sit in Singapore, when disconnecting helps or causes problems, and what to check before you fly.`,lead:`A car left parked for two or three weeks can come back with a flat battery. Disconnecting the battery is the answer people suggest most often, but on many modern cars it is not the best one. Here is how to decide.`,sections:[{h:`Why a parked car drains its battery`,p:[`Even when the engine is off, a modern car keeps drawing a small current. The alarm, the central locking receiver, the engine computer memory, a keyless-entry receiver and any dashcam or tracker all take a little power. On most cars this is a small trickle, but over weeks it adds up.`,`Singapore's heat also works against the battery. A battery that is already two or three years old has less reserve, so the same trickle that a new battery shrugs off for a month can flatten an older one in two or three weeks. There is no exact number that applies to every car, which is why the age and condition of the battery matter more than the calendar.`]},{h:`How long can a car sit before it is a risk`,p:[`As a rough guide, a healthy battery under about three years old in a car with no extra electronics will usually cope with a trip of up to two weeks. Beyond three weeks, or with an older battery, a car that will not start on return becomes a real possibility. Cars with a dashcam in parking mode, a tracker, or a keyless system that keeps listening for the key are more likely to drain sooner.`,`These are general guidelines, not promises. If your battery is already slow to crank, treat it as the weak link before you leave, not after you return.`]},{h:`Should you disconnect the battery?`,p:[`Disconnecting the negative terminal does stop the drain, and on an older, simpler car it is a reasonable choice for a long absence. On many modern cars it causes other problems. Radio presets, the clock, window and sunroof auto-close settings and the engine's learned idle values can reset. Some cars show warning lights on the next start. Many European cars, and some other makes, need a new or reconnected battery to be registered with the car's computer, and a few will not behave properly until that is done.`,`Also, an electronic handbrake, an electric tailgate or a keyless system can make a disconnected car awkward to move or open. Check your owner's manual first. If you are unsure, we would rather you do not disconnect it yourself, and ask for help instead.`]},{h:`Safer alternatives to disconnecting`,p:[`Before you leave, switch off anything that is not needed: turn off dashcam parking mode, unplug phone chargers and any OBD or tracker devices that you do not need, and make sure the boot, doors and lights are fully shut. Keep the key fob well away from the car, because some keyless cars keep communicating with a fob that is close by.`,`If a friend or family member can start the car and drive it for at least 20 to 30 minutes once a week, that does more than idling in the carpark. A short start that does not recharge what it used can make the situation worse. A battery maintainer is the neatest option if you have a power point at your parking space, but most carparks do not.`]},{h:`What to do before you fly`,p:[`Have the battery tested if it is more than three years old, if the engine has been slow to start, or if the car has a start-stop system. A test takes only a few minutes and tells you whether the battery is likely to survive the trip. If it is weak, it is cheaper and less stressful to replace it before you travel than to deal with a dead car at the airport carpark on your return.`,`If you do come back to a flat battery, do not keep trying to crank the engine. Send us a message on WhatsApp with your location, your car model and year, and a photo of the battery label if you can reach it safely. Our reply is not instant, because the person in charge replies personally, so if you are in an unsafe spot, call your roadside assistance first.`]}],faqs:[{q:`How long can I leave my car without starting it?`,a:`A healthy battery under three years old will usually manage up to about two weeks. After three weeks, or with an older battery, a car that will not start becomes much more likely.`},{q:`Is it safe to disconnect my car battery myself?`,a:`It can be fine on an older, simple car, but on many modern cars it resets settings or needs the battery to be registered with the car's computer. If you are unsure, ask first, and check your owner's manual.`},{q:`Does starting the car and idling in the carpark help?`,a:`Not much. A short idle does not put back the charge the start used. A drive of 20 to 30 minutes is more useful.`},{q:`Should I replace an old battery before a long trip?`,a:`If it is more than three years old or the engine has been slow to start, getting it tested before you leave is a sensible step, because replacing it on your own schedule is easier than dealing with a flat battery on return.`}]},{slug:`what-should-a-car-battery-quote-include-singapore`,title:`What Should a Car Battery Quote Include in Singapore?`,short:`A checklist so two battery quotes can be compared fairly`,description:`Two car battery quotes can look very different and still not be comparable. What a fair quote should state: exact battery, fitting, old battery disposal, registration or coding, GST and warranty.`,lead:`Two quotes for a car battery can differ by a lot, and the cheaper-looking one is not always cheaper once the extras are added. Here is what to check so you compare like with like.`,sections:[{h:`Start with the exact battery, not just the car`,p:[`A quote should name the brand, the model line and the battery code, for example 55B24L or LN3, not just 'battery for your car'. The same car can take more than one battery depending on the year, the engine and whether it has a start-stop system. If a quote does not say which battery it is, you cannot tell what you are being offered. Our guide on battery codes explains how to read the label on your current battery.`]},{h:`Standard, EFB or AGM: is it the right type for your car?`,p:[`A car with a start-stop system normally needs an EFB or AGM battery. Fitting a cheaper standard battery in place of one can shorten its life. If the quote is lower because the type is lower, that is not a like-for-like saving. Our guide on EFB, AGM and standard batteries explains the difference.`]},{h:`Is fitting included, and where is it done?`,p:[`Some quotes show the battery alone and add fitting later. Ask whether the price covers removing the old battery, installing the new one and checking that the car starts and charges. Also ask whether the work is done at a workshop or where your car is parked, and whether that changes the price.`]},{h:`What happens to the old battery?`,p:[`Used car batteries must be disposed of properly, so a fair quote says whether the old battery is taken away and whether that is already in the price. Some sellers give a trade-in credit for the old battery. Ask whether the figure you were given is before or after any such credit.`]},{h:`European cars: registration or coding`,p:[`Many European cars need the new battery to be registered with the car's electronics after a replacement. If your car is one of them, ask whether registration or coding is included, who does it, and what it costs if it is extra. Our guide on European car battery registration explains why this step matters.`]},{h:`GST, warranty and what is extra`,p:[`Ask whether the price already includes GST, because a quote before GST and a quote after GST are not comparable. Ask how long the warranty is, what it covers and what it does not, and what you need to show to make a claim. Our guide on battery warranty questions lists what to ask. Keep the terms in writing, and be careful with any promise that sounds too good to be true.`]},{h:`How to get a quote you can compare`,p:[`Take a clear photo of the label on your current battery and note your car's make, model and year. With those details a seller can tell you the exact battery, what is included and what is extra. Send the photo to us on WhatsApp and we will confirm the right battery and quote you.`]}],faqs:[{q:`Why do two quotes for the same car differ so much?`,a:`They are often not for the same thing. The battery brand, the battery type (standard, EFB or AGM), whether fitting and old battery disposal are included, and the warranty can all differ. Compare the battery code and the list of what is included before comparing the price.`},{q:`Should the old battery be taken away in the quoted price?`,a:`A fair quote says what happens to the old battery. Ask whether disposal is included and whether any trade-in credit has already been taken off the price.`},{q:`Do I need to register the new battery on my car?`,a:`Many European cars do, and some others with a battery management system may as well. Check your car's requirements and ask whether registration or coding is included in the quote.`}]},{slug:`car-battery-draining-overnight-causes`,title:`Car Battery Draining Overnight: Causes and How to Find the Drain`,short:`Why a battery goes flat overnight and how to test for a drain`,description:`Why a car battery goes flat overnight or after a few days parked: parasitic drain, short trips, dashcams and a failing battery, and how to test for a drain at home.`,lead:`If the car starts fine in the evening and is dead in the morning, either the battery can no longer hold a charge or something is drawing power while the car is off. Here is how to tell which, and what to check first.`,sections:[{h:`Two different problems`,p:[`A battery that drains overnight usually falls into one of two groups. In the first, the battery itself is worn out and cannot hold a charge, so even a small normal draw empties it. In the second, something in the car is using too much current while it is parked, which is called a parasitic drain. The fix is different, so it helps to know which one you have.`,`A battery older than about three to five years, one that struggles on short trips, and one that has been flat more than once are all signs of the first group.`]},{h:`Common causes of a parasitic drain`,p:[`A modern car is never fully off. The alarm, the central locking and the clock use a very small current, and the electronics go to sleep a few minutes after you lock the car. A problem is when something keeps them awake or draws more power than it should.`,`Typical culprits are a boot light, glovebox light or vanity mirror light that stays on, a door that does not register as shut, a hard-wired dashcam in parking mode, a phone charger or USB device left in, an aftermarket alarm or audio amplifier, and a faulty module that does not go to sleep. A failing alternator can also drain the battery if its diode lets current flow backward.`]},{h:`Short trips and long gaps`,p:[`A battery can also run down with no fault at all. In Singapore, many cars do short stop-start trips that never give the alternator long enough to refill the battery after starting. Add a long gap, such as a week of the car sitting in a car park, or a holiday overseas, and the battery slowly loses charge. If that sounds like your car, a longer drive every week helps. For a long trip abroad, see our guide on whether to disconnect the battery before travelling.`]},{h:`How to test for a drain at home`,p:[`The test needs a multimeter that can read current in amps. Switch the car off, close the doors and wait about 30 minutes for the electronics to go to sleep. Loosen the negative battery clamp, and connect the meter in series between the clamp and the negative terminal, so that the car's current flows through the meter. A healthy modern car typically shows a current in the tens of milliamps once asleep. A reading of hundreds of milliamps, or amps, means something is drawing too much.`,`To find the circuit, pull the fuses one at a time and watch the reading drop. The circuit that makes the current fall is where the problem is. Take care: do not start the car or open and close doors with the meter connected, and if the car has battery registration or a start-stop system, ask a workshop before disconnecting anything.`]},{h:`When to replace the battery`,p:[`If the drain test is normal and the battery still goes flat, the battery is the problem. A workshop can test its capacity. If it needs replacing, the replacement has to match the type your car uses, which is shown on the label. Send us a photo of the label and your car model and we will tell you what fits.`]}],faqs:[{q:`How long can a car sit before the battery goes flat?`,a:`It depends on the battery and the car. A healthy battery in a car with a normal draw can sit for a few weeks, but an older battery, or a car with a dashcam or alarm, may not last a week.`},{q:`Will jump-starting fix a battery that drains overnight?`,a:`It gets you moving, but if the cause is a drain or a worn battery it will go flat again. Find the cause.`},{q:`Can a dashcam drain the battery?`,a:`Yes, if it is hard-wired and records in parking mode. Many have a low-voltage cut-off, which should be set correctly.`},{q:`Is it the alternator or the battery?`,a:`If the battery is flat after the car has been driven for a long trip, suspect the alternator or the battery. A workshop can test charging voltage in a few minutes.`}]},{slug:`car-battery-terminal-corrosion-causes-and-cleaning`,title:`Car Battery Terminal Corrosion: What the White Powder Means and How to Clean It`,short:`Causes, safe cleaning steps and when corrosion means a new battery`,description:`What the white, blue or green crust on a car battery terminal is, what causes it in Singapore's humid weather, how to clean it safely and when it points to a failing battery.`,lead:`A white or blue-green crust on the battery terminals is common, and it can stop a car from starting even when the battery is healthy. Here is what it is, how to clean it safely and what it can tell you about the battery.`,sections:[{h:`What the crust is`,p:[`The powder on the terminals is a build-up of salts formed when the acid vapour or a tiny leak from the battery meets the metal of the terminal and clamp, and the damp air. White or grey powder is usually lead sulphate on a lead terminal. Blue or green crust is usually copper sulphate, from the copper in the clamp or cable end.`,`The crust is a poor conductor. It adds resistance between the battery and the cable, so the car can crank slowly or fail to start, or the charging can be weak, even though the battery itself is fine.`]},{h:`Why it happens`,p:[`A small amount of corrosion over time is normal, and humid weather speeds it up. Heavier corrosion points to a cause: a loose clamp that lets vapour escape, a cracked case or a leaking seal around the terminal post, overcharging that makes the battery vent more, or an old battery nearing the end of its life.`,`If one terminal is much worse than the other, or the case looks swollen or wet, the battery may be leaking, and cleaning will not fix it.`]},{h:`How to clean it safely`,p:[`Wear gloves and eye protection, and keep sparks and flames away. Switch the car off. Loosen and remove the negative clamp first, then the positive. Mix a spoonful of bicarbonate of soda in a little warm water and brush it onto the terminals and clamps with an old toothbrush or a terminal brush. It will fizz as it neutralises the acid. Wipe it off with a damp cloth, taking care not to let the water run into the battery vents, and dry everything.`,`Reconnect the positive clamp first and the negative last, and make sure both are tight and do not move by hand. Finish with a thin layer of terminal grease or an anti-corrosion spray to slow the crust returning.`]},{h:`Check the car's requirements first`,p:[`On many newer and European cars, disconnecting the battery can reset electronics, and some cars need the new or reconnected battery registered to the charging system. If your car is one of these, or has a start-stop system, ask a workshop to do it. Our guide on battery registration for European cars explains why.`]},{h:`When corrosion means a new battery`,p:[`If the crust comes back within weeks, if the case is swollen or leaking, or if the car is slow to start even after the terminals are clean and tight, have the battery tested. A leaking battery should be replaced, not cleaned. Send us a photo of the battery label and your car model and we will tell you what fits.`]}],faqs:[{q:`Can I use hot water or cola?`,a:`Warm water and bicarbonate of soda is the standard method. Soft drinks leave sugar behind, which is a bad idea near electrical connections.`},{q:`Is battery corrosion dangerous to touch?`,a:`It can irritate the skin and eyes, so wear gloves and eye protection and wash your hands afterwards.`},{q:`Does corrosion mean the battery is dead?`,a:`No. It often means only that the connection is poor. Clean it and see whether the symptoms stop.`},{q:`How often should I check the terminals?`,a:`A look every few months, and always before a long trip, is enough.`}]},{slug:`car-battery-not-charging-alternator-belt-or-battery`,title:`Car Battery Not Charging: Alternator, Belt or Battery? How to Tell`,short:`The warning light, a quick voltage check and what usually fails`,description:`How to tell whether a car battery is not charging because of the alternator, the drive belt, loose connections or a worn battery, with the warning signs and a simple voltage check.`,lead:`A battery that keeps going flat is either not being charged properly or cannot hold the charge it gets. The alternator, the belt that drives it, the connections and the battery itself are the four things to separate.`,sections:[{h:`The signs`,p:[`The battery warning light on the dashboard is the clearest sign that the charging system is not working. Other signs are headlights and the dashboard dimming while the engine is running, a car that starts after a jump but is flat again a short while later, and electrical items such as windows or the radio behaving slowly or oddly.`,`If the light comes on while you are driving, switch off any unneeded electrical items and head for a workshop. The car is running on the battery alone and will eventually stop.`]},{h:`What the alternator does`,p:[`The battery starts the engine. Once the engine is running, the alternator takes over: it powers the car's electrical system and recharges the battery. If the alternator is failing, the battery is the only supply and is slowly emptied, however good the battery is. That is why replacing a battery without checking the charging system sometimes gives a second flat battery a few weeks later.`]},{h:`A simple voltage check`,p:[`A multimeter set to DC volts gives a quick answer. With the engine off and the car rested for a few hours, a healthy battery usually reads around 12.4 to 12.7 volts. A reading below about 12.0 volts means it is quite flat. With the engine running, the charging system normally raises the reading to somewhere around 13.5 to 14.5 volts. If the reading with the engine running stays at the resting level, the alternator is not charging. If it is far higher than the normal range, the regulator may be faulty and overcharging the battery.`,`These are typical ranges, so check your handbook for your car. Take care near moving parts, and do not touch the belts or pulleys with the engine running.`]},{h:`Other causes to check`,p:[`A slipping or broken drive belt, which you may hear as a squeal, can stop the alternator turning. Loose or corroded battery terminals, a worn earth strap and damaged wiring all reduce the charge that gets through. Our guide on terminal corrosion shows how to clean them.`,`On some modern cars a smart charging system deliberately holds back charge at times, so a lower reading in some conditions is not always a fault. A workshop can test it with a scan tool.`]},{h:`When the battery is the problem`,p:[`If the charging voltage is normal and the battery is still flat, the battery is probably worn. A battery that is three to five years old, has been flat several times or is slow to crank should be tested. A workshop can check its capacity in a few minutes. If it needs replacing, send us a photo of the label and your car model and we will tell you what fits. For cars with start-stop or registration, see our guides on the battery types and on European battery registration.`]}],faqs:[{q:`Can a bad battery damage the alternator?`,a:`A very weak battery makes the alternator work harder, which shortens its life. Fix it early.`},{q:`Can I drive with the battery light on?`,a:`Only to get somewhere safe. The car is running on the battery and will stop once it is empty.`},{q:`Will a jump start fix it?`,a:`It gets the engine going. If the charging fault remains, the battery will go flat again.`},{q:`How long does an alternator last?`,a:`There is no fixed figure. It depends on the car and use, and many last for years before they need attention.`}]},{slug:`sealion-7-vs-model-y-weekend-charging`,noindex:!0,title:`BYD Sealion 7 Premium vs Tesla Model Y: Weekdays in Singapore, Weekend Charging in Johor`,short:`Which electric SUV suits a short weekday routine and a weekend home charge in Malaysia`,description:`BYD Sealion 7 Premium or Tesla Model Y for a driver who stays in Singapore on weekdays and charges at home in Johor at the weekend? Price, range, ride, noise and charging compared.`,lead:`Picture a simple week: short drives in Singapore from Monday to Friday, then a weekend at a home in Johor where you can install a charger. Two popular electric SUVs fit that routine, the BYD Sealion 7 Premium and the Tesla Model Y. Here is how they compare, using published prices, specifications and reviews. Prices and COE move every two weeks, so treat the numbers as a snapshot from November 2026.`,sections:[{h:`The short answer`,p:[`For a routine of about 200 km a week with a weekend charge at home, both cars have more range than you need. That makes price, ride, noise and everyday features matter more than the biggest range figure.`,`The Sealion 7 Premium costs less and supports Apple CarPlay and Android Auto. The Model Y rides more tightly, is quicker, and has Tesla's own charging and service network around Johor. It is also the noisier of the two on the road, according to several reviews.`]},{h:`Price and range side by side`,p:[`BYD Singapore advertises the Sealion 7 Premium with COE, and a Motorist review lists it in VES Band A1. Its battery is 82.5 kWh with a 480 km WLTP range, and BYD quotes a 10-year or 200,000 km battery warranty.`,`Tesla's Singapore line-up in an August 2026 dealer price list ran from the Premium RWD (62.5 kWh) to the Long Range RWD (84 kWh, up to 661 km WLTP) and the six-seat Model Y L (88 kWh, 681 km). In the listings we compared, the Model Y Long Range was roughly 12 to 22 percent more expensive than the Sealion 7 Premium, for about 180 km more rated range, and the Model Y L was roughly a quarter to a third more.`],list:[`Sealion 7 Premium: 82.5 kWh, 480 km WLTP, about 230 kW, 0 to 100 km/h in about 6.7 seconds.`,`Model Y Long Range RWD: 84 kWh, up to 661 km WLTP.`,`UK road tests put the Model Y RWD at about 5.9 seconds to 100 km/h, against 6.7 for the Sealion 7.`]},{h:`How they drive`,p:[`Reviews describe the Model Y as firm but composed, with less body lean and a more agile feel. The Sealion 7 is softer; one review called its ride wallowy as well as firm, and reviewers noticed the body leaning in corners.`,`Road noise is the most repeated complaint about the Model Y, including in Singapore reviews, with one noting noise from the tyres at around 80 km/h. Later cars with revised suspension are reported to be quieter. We did not find a comparable noise comment for the Sealion 7, so that is not a verdict either way.`,`The Model Y's regenerative braking cannot be switched off completely. It has a hold mode for one-pedal driving, but small speed adjustments by lifting off the accelerator take some getting used to.`]},{h:`Everyday features`,p:[`The Sealion 7 supports Apple CarPlay and Android Auto. The Model Y does not support either, so navigation and music run through Tesla's own system.`,`BYD lists a nine-airbag safety package and a 360-degree camera for the Sealion 7, and 6/4 split-fold rear seats with up to 1,789 litres of cargo space. The Sealion 7 measures 4,830 mm long and 1,925 mm wide. We did not find comparable Model Y dimensions and boot figures from a source we trust, so check those on a test drive.`]},{h:`Charging between Johor and Singapore`,p:[`At a landed home in Johor, charging is the same for both cars: a wallbox on a dedicated circuit. Published guides put a 7 kW single-phase installation at roughly RM3,500 to RM8,000, and a TNB supply upgrade at RM200 to RM3,500 if the existing supply is too small. Domestic TNB electricity is about 27 to 28 sen per kWh, and a time-of-use plan has off-peak rates of about 24 sen.`,`As an illustration, a car that uses 18 kWh per 100 km needs about 36 kWh for 200 km, which a 7 kW charger delivers in about five hours. Your own car's efficiency will differ.`,`Tesla owners also have Superchargers in Johor, including Sunway Big Box in Iskandar Puteri, and Tesla has announced shared service coverage across Malaysia, Singapore and Thailand. We did not find a source confirming whether other brands can use Tesla Superchargers in Malaysia, so check before you rely on it.`]},{h:`Which one suits which driver`,p:[`Choose by how you actually drive.`],list:[`Pick the Sealion 7 Premium if you want a lower price, CarPlay or Android Auto, and a softer ride for mostly short trips.`,`Pick the Model Y if you value a tighter, faster feel, expect longer trips where Tesla's chargers help, and can live with more road noise.`,`Test drive both on a road you know. Ride and noise are personal.`]},{h:`After you choose: tyres and the 12V battery`,p:[`Whichever car you pick, two things will need replacing at some point: the tyres and the small 12V battery. We supply tyres and car batteries, and we do not sell cars. Tyres are quoted on WhatsApp once the size is confirmed from a photo of the tyre sidewall.`,`Electric cars and hybrids still carry an ordinary 12V battery that runs the computers and wakes the high-voltage system. If it fails, the car can refuse to start even with a full main battery. Which 12V battery fits depends on the model and build year, and some newer cars use a lithium type that is a different product. Send your car model and a photo of the label and we will tell you honestly whether it is something we supply.`]}],faqs:[{q:`Is the Sealion 7 Premium cheaper than the Model Y in Singapore?`,a:`In the listings we compared, yes: the Sealion 7 Premium was below every Model Y variant we found except possibly the entry Premium RWD, where the gap was small. COE changes every two weeks, so ask for a current quote with COE.`},{q:`Which has the longer range?`,a:`The Model Y Long Range RWD is rated up to 661 km WLTP against 480 km for the Sealion 7 Premium. For about 200 km a week, both are more than enough.`},{q:`Does the Model Y support Apple CarPlay?`,a:`No. The Sealion 7 supports Apple CarPlay and Android Auto, while the Model Y does not support either.`},{q:`Can I charge at home in Johor with a Singapore-registered car?`,a:`We did not find a rule that stops it. Charging at your own home uses a home charger on your own supply, and the installer will check the supply and the approvals.`}],images:[{src:`/blog/sealion-7-front.webp`,alt:`BYD Sealion 7 seen from the front left`,w:1e3,h:667,after:0,cap:`BYD Sealion 7 · front`,credit:`Alexander Migl / Wikimedia Commons, CC BY-SA 4.0`,url:`https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:BYD_Sealion_07_EV_DSC_8264.jpg`,licenseUrl:`https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/`},{src:`/blog/model-y-front.webp`,alt:`Tesla Model Y seen from the front left`,w:1e3,h:667,after:0,cap:`Tesla Model Y · front`,credit:`Damian B Oh / Wikimedia Commons, CC BY-SA 4.0`,url:`https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tesla_Model_Y_Dual_Motor_First_Edition_Juniper_Quicksilver_(6).jpg`,licenseUrl:`https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/`},{src:`/blog/sealion-7-rear.webp`,alt:`BYD Sealion 7 seen from the rear left`,w:1e3,h:667,after:2,cap:`BYD Sealion 7 · rear`,credit:`Alexander Migl / Wikimedia Commons, CC BY-SA 4.0`,url:`https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:BYD_Sealion_07_EV_DSC_8259.jpg`,licenseUrl:`https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/`},{src:`/blog/model-y-rear.webp`,alt:`Tesla Model Y seen from the rear left`,w:1e3,h:667,after:2,cap:`Tesla Model Y · rear`,credit:`Damian B Oh / Wikimedia Commons, CC BY-SA 4.0`,url:`https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tesla_Model_Y_Dual_Motor_First_Edition_Juniper_Quicksilver_(15).jpg`,licenseUrl:`https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/`},{src:`/blog/sealion-7-interior.webp`,alt:`BYD Sealion 7 dashboard and centre console`,w:1e3,h:667,after:3,cap:`BYD Sealion 7 · cabin`,credit:`Ethan Llamas / Wikimedia Commons, CC BY-SA 4.0`,url:`https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:BYD_Sealion_7_Performance_AWD_interior.jpg`,licenseUrl:`https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/`},{src:`/blog/model-y-interior.webp`,alt:`Tesla Model Y dashboard and steering wheel`,w:1e3,h:667,after:3,cap:`Tesla Model Y · cabin`,credit:`Ethan Llamas / Wikimedia Commons, CC BY-SA 4.0`,url:`https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tesla_Model_Y_2025_interior.jpg`,licenseUrl:`https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/`}],waMessage:`Hi {brand}, I'm looking at a BYD Sealion 7 or Tesla Model Y. I'll send my car model and a photo of the tyre sidewall or the 12V battery label so you can confirm the tyre size or what battery fits.`,waLabel:`Own one, or about to? Send your car model on WhatsApp`},{slug:`car-battery-replacement-singapore-what-to-expect`,title:`Car Battery Replacement in Singapore: What to Expect Step by Step`,short:`From the first warning sign to a battery that fits and starts the car`,description:`What happens when you replace a car battery in Singapore: warning signs, what to prepare, how the right battery is chosen, registration for European cars, old battery disposal and warranty questions.`,lead:`Replacing a car battery sounds simple, but the right battery depends on your car, and a few small details decide whether the new one lasts. This is the order things normally happen in, and what to prepare so nothing is guessed.`,sections:[{h:`1. Notice the warning signs early`,p:[`A battery rarely fails without warning. The engine may crank more slowly than before, the headlights may dim when you start the car or switch on the air-conditioning, or the charging warning light may come on. A swollen case or white or blue crust on the terminals is also a sign to have the battery looked at. Our guide on how long car batteries last in Singapore lists the signs in more detail.`]},{h:`2. Prepare the details a seller needs`,p:[`To name the right battery, a seller needs your car's make, model and year, and a clear photo of the label on the battery that is in the car now. The label shows the code, the type and often the terminal layout. The same car can take different batteries depending on the year and engine, so the car's name on its own is not enough.`]},{h:`3. The right size and the right type`,p:[`Battery codes such as 55B24L or LN3 tell you the size and the terminal position, and the last letter, L or R, matters because the wrong one may not fit. Our guide on battery codes explains how to read them.`,`Type matters as much as size. A car with a start-stop system normally needs an EFB or AGM battery, and a standard battery in its place can wear out early. The rule is the same type or better, never lower.`]},{h:`4. European cars: registration or coding`,p:[`Many European cars need the new battery to be registered with the car's electronics after it is fitted, so the charging system treats it correctly. Without it, a new battery can be undercharged and wear out sooner. If your car is European, ask before you agree whether registration is included. Our guide on European car battery registration explains why.`]},{h:`5. Fitting and checking`,p:[`A proper replacement removes the old battery, cleans the terminal clamps, fits the new battery securely, and then checks that the car starts and that the charging system is working. Ask whether these steps are included and where the work is done, because that can change what you pay.`]},{h:`6. The old battery and the paperwork`,p:[`Used car batteries must be disposed of properly, so ask whether the old battery is taken away. Keep a written record of the battery model and the warranty terms. Our guide on battery warranty questions lists what to ask, and our guide on what a battery quote should include helps you compare quotes fairly.`]},{h:`Not sure which battery your car needs?`,p:[`Send us your car model and a photo of the battery label on WhatsApp. We confirm the right battery first, then give you a quote that states what is included.`]}],faqs:[{q:`How do I know my car needs a new battery and not something else?`,a:`Slow cranking, dim lights and a charging warning light often point to the battery, but a charging fault can look similar. A car that cranks strongly but will not start usually has a different problem. Our guide on a car that will not start explains the checks.`},{q:`Can I fit any battery that is the right size?`,a:`Not always. Size is only part of it. The type must also suit your car, for example EFB or AGM for start-stop cars, and a European car may need the new battery registered.`},{q:`What should I send to get the right battery quoted?`,a:`Your car's make, model and year, and a clear photo of the label on your current battery.`}]}]},n=e.cars;function r(e,t){return n.find(n=>n.makeSlug===e&&n.modelSlug===t)}function i(e,t=6){return n.filter(t=>t.makeSlug===e.makeSlug&&t.modelSlug!==e.modelSlug).slice(0,t)}function a(e){let t=/^(?:\d+)?([A-Z]{1,3}\d{2})/.exec(e)??/^(DIN\d+)/.exec(e);return t?t[1]:e}function o(){let e=new Map;for(let t of n){let n=e.get(t.makeSlug)??{make:t.make,makeSlug:t.makeSlug,cars:[]};n.cars.push(t),e.set(t.makeSlug,n)}return[...e.values()].sort((e,t)=>e.make.localeCompare(t.make))}function s(e,t){return`Hi ${e}, I'd like a car battery quote${t?` for my ${t}`:``}. I'll send a photo of the label on my current battery so you can confirm the right one.`}function c(){return t.guides}function l(e){return c().find(t=>t.slug===e)}var u={"battery-size-codes-explained":[{slug:`efb-agm-or-standard-battery`,why:`The code also tells you whether your car needs a stop-start (EFB or AGM) battery.`},{slug:`warranty-questions-for-car-batteries`,why:`Once you know the size, check what the warranty should cover.`}],"efb-agm-or-standard-battery":[{slug:`battery-size-codes-explained`,why:`How to read the size code printed on your battery.`},{slug:`european-car-battery-registration`,why:`AGM and EFB batteries in European cars often need registering with the car.`}],"car-wont-start-battery-or-not":[{slug:`how-long-car-batteries-last-singapore`,why:`If the battery is more than three years old, this explains what to expect.`},{slug:`battery-size-codes-explained`,why:`Before you ask for a replacement, know what to look for on the label.`}],"how-long-car-batteries-last-singapore":[{slug:`car-wont-start-battery-or-not`,why:`A checklist if your car is already slow to start.`},{slug:`disconnect-car-battery-before-overseas-trip`,why:`Leaving the car parked for weeks? This is what shortens battery life most.`}],"european-car-battery-registration":[{slug:`efb-agm-or-standard-battery`,why:`Which battery type your car needs before it is registered.`},{slug:`warranty-questions-for-car-batteries`,why:`What to ask about warranty on a battery that needs coding.`}],"warranty-questions-for-car-batteries":[{slug:`battery-size-codes-explained`,why:`Make sure the battery you are quoted is the right size first.`},{slug:`how-long-car-batteries-last-singapore`,why:`What battery life to expect in Singapore.`}],"forklift-lorry-and-factory-vehicle-batteries":[{slug:`battery-size-codes-explained`,why:`Commercial batteries use the same kind of size code.`},{slug:`warranty-questions-for-car-batteries`,why:`Warranty questions that matter more when several vehicles are involved.`}],"disconnect-car-battery-before-overseas-trip":[{slug:`how-long-car-batteries-last-singapore`,why:`How long a battery lasts, and how to tell if yours is already weak.`},{slug:`car-wont-start-battery-or-not`,why:`What to check if the car will not start when you come back.`},{slug:`european-car-battery-registration`,why:`Why European cars are more sensitive to disconnecting the battery.`}]};export{l as a,i as c,r as i,e as l,s as n,c as o,a as r,o as s,u as t};